# Cultural Landscape Report for Gettysburg National Military Park: Record of Treatment, Volume I (Layton, Chinburg, Braquet, Brown; OCLP 2018).
# Quotes are typed in the corrected spelling ('=' form; the text layer lost the i of fi ligatures: feld, fled, ofered); the builder checks every letter.
# Each treatment entry has a heading (the fence line, field, orchard or wood and its GIS ids) followed by the 1863 description and, last, the rebuilding done 1998-2013.
# 'Documented in the 1863 Bachelder map / 1868 Warren map / 1872 survey / photographs' is the report's own statement of its documentation; those maps date from 1863-1872
# and the photographs from 1863-1900, so each is carried as the report's conclusion about the July 1863 line, not as an 1863 survey.
@ NPS-CLR-ROT-V1
%basis the report's endnotes: damage claims (NARA RG 92; Pennsylvania Board of Claims 1871-79), the 1863 Bachelder map, the 1863 Cope and Elliott maps, the 1868 Warren map, the 1872 topographical survey, photographs (Tipton, Boardman and park collections)

# --- Treatment Philosophy: The 1863 Landscape (2004), reproduced in the Battlefield Rehabilitation Framework, PDF pp. 69-76 ---
69 | fields | 1863 farm fields: usually under 15 acres | - | 1863-07 | stated | Most farm fields around Gettysburg were under 15 acres, owned by subsistence farmers or leased to tenants, each farmer dividing land into pasture, grain for feed, crops for food or sale and woodlots | =Most farm fields surrounding Gettysburg were less than 15 acres and owned by local subsistence farmers or leased to tenant farmers.
69 | fields | Cemetery Hill: open fields with low crops | - | 1863-07 | stated | The open fields with low crops on Cemetery Hill allowed excellent observation from this Union stronghold | =For example, the open fields with low crops on Cemetery Hill allowed for excellent observation from this Union stronghold.
70 | buildings | Farmstead cluster: house, barn and the usual outbuildings | - | 1863-07 | stated | A farmstead cluster usually had the farmhouse and barn and some or all of a wagon shed, corn crib, pig pen, chicken coop, smokehouse, blacksmith shop, spring house, summer kitchen, wood shed, privy, dooryard and barnyard fencing, ornamental and shade trees, orchards and gardens | =Farmstead clusters usually included the main farm house and a barn, and some or all of the following: a wagon shed, corn crib, pig pen, chicken coop, smokehouse, blacksmith shop, spring house, summer kitchen, wood shed, privy, dooryard fencing, barnyard fencing, ornamental and shade trees, orchards, and gardens.
71 | woodlots | Woodlots: deciduous, on the poorest land | - | 1863-07 | stated | Most woodlots were deciduous and typically on the poorest land with rocky, thin or steeply sloped soil, supplying fencing, firewood, housing and general carpentry | =Most woodlots consisted of deciduous species.
71 | woodlots | Woodlots and groves on poor land | - | 1863-07 | stated | The 1860s subsistence farms around Gettysburg included managed woodlots, groves, hedgerows, orchards, nurseries and individual ornamental and shade trees | =The 1860s subsistence farms that surrounded Gettysburg included managed vegetation, such as woodlots, groves, hedgerows, orchards, nurseries, and individual ornamental and shade trees.
72 | woodlots | Hedgerows mostly unmanaged growth along fences | - | 1863-07 | stated | Most hedgerows were the unmanaged growth of woody vegetation along fence lines or on poor rocky soil and boulder outcrops, made of various trees, shrubs and briars; only occasionally were they planted as living fences | =Most hedgerows, however, were the result of natural and unmanaged growth of woody vegetation along fence lines or areas of poor rocky soil or boulder outcroppings.
72 | orchards | Many orchards dotted the 1863 landscape | - | 1863-07 | stated | Many orchards dotted the 1863 landscape because tree fruit fed subsistence farmers and their livestock | =Many orchards dotted the 1863 landscape because tree fruits provided a valuable food source for subsistence farmers and for livestock.
73 | orchards | Species of orchard trees did not affect the fighting | - | 1863-07 | stated | The species of orchard trees had no effect on the fighting because most fruit trees have similar size, foliage and cover | =The species of orchard trees had no impact upon the fighting, because most fruit trees have similar characteristics (tree size, foliage, and cover).
73 | orchards | Replanting spacing used today: 40 ft on centre, Sherfy 20 ft | FEN-sherfy-orchard | 2000s replanting (modern prescription) | stated | The park replants orchards 40 feet on centre, except the Sherfy Peach Orchard where trees are 20 feet on centre | =Plant trees 40 feet on center, with the exception of the Sherfy Peach Orchard, where trees are 20 feet on center.
73 | woodlots | Individual trees in dooryards, fields, fence lines and boundary lines | - | 1863-07 | stated | Individual trees dotted the 1863 battlefield, many in dooryards, in fields near rock outcrops, along fence lines and along boundary lines | =Individual trees dotted the 1863 Gettysburg battlefield, many of which were located in dooryards, in fields near rock outcroppings, along fence lines, and along boundary lines.
74 | fences | The Fence Law of 1700 | - | 1700-1863 | stated | The Fence Law of 1700 required all crop fields to be enclosed to keep out wandering livestock, with fences at least five feet high, of sufficient rail or logs, and close at the bottom | =Mandated by law, the Fence Law of 1700 stated that all crop fields be enclosed to keep out wandering livestock and that all fences should be, “at least five feet high, of sufficient rail or logs, and close at the bottom.”
74 | fences | 1863 fence materials: wood or wood and stone, black locust and American chestnut | - | 1863-07 | stated | The fences of the 1863 Gettysburg landscape were mostly wood or wood and stone, and predominantly black locust and American chestnut | =The fences of the 1863 Gettysburg landscape were mostly wood or wood and stone, and predominantly black locust and American chestnut.
74 | fences | Farmers north of town: all-wood fences; south of town: wood and stone or stone | - | 1863-07 | stated | Farmers north of Gettysburg were wealthier with larger farms and could afford all-wood fences including mortised rail and board; farmers south of the town had smaller, rockier farms and so fences of wood and stone or entirely of stone | =The farmers to the north of Gettysburg were generally wealthier, owned larger farms, and could afford fences made entirely of wood, including mortised rail and board.
74 | fences | Eight types or styles of fence in the 1863 landscape | - | 1863-07 | stated | Eight types or styles of fences were present in the 1863 Gettysburg landscape, each with its own materials, construction, strength, tightness, height and mass | =Eight types or styles of fences were present in the 1863 Gettysburg landscape, each with distinguishable materials, construction methods, strength, tightness, height, and mass.
74 | fences | Fences slowed troops and were torn down for breastworks | - | 1863-07 | stated | Fences were a major obstacle that slowed troops, who had to dismantle or climb them, they gave cover, and some were torn down to build breastworks | =Fences proved to be a major obstacle that slowed the movement of troops in battle, because they needed to be either dismantled or climbed.
75 | fences | 39 miles of 1863 fences lost to modern agriculture (GMP 1999) | - | 1999 estimate (modern) | stated | In 1999 the park's GMP estimated that 39 miles of 1863 fences had been lost to modern agricultural practice; the fences that remained did not reflect the height, mass and tightness of the 1863 fences | =In 1999, the park’s GMP estimated that 39 miles of 1863 fences had been lost to modern agricultural practices within the preserved battlefield landscape.
75 | fences | Where no documentation exists but a map shows a fence: simple three-rail fence | - | 2004 treatment rule (modern) | stated | Where no documentation exists but historic maps note a fence, the park installs a simple fence such as three-rail post and rail to mark its presence, so such lines are not evidence of the 1863 fence type | =When no documentation is available, but historic maps note the presence of a fence, the park will install a simple fence, such as three-rail post and rail, to indicate the presence of a fence.
75 | fences | Two prevalent gate styles: hinged and removable rails | - | 1863-07 | stated | Two styles of gates, hinged and removable rails, were prevalent; removable rails were two posts bored with two rows of holes to take four or five rails | =Two styles of gates, hinged and removable rails, were prevalent.
76 | buildings | Wells: most farms had a hand-dug stone-lined well | - | 1863-07 | stated | Most farms had at least one hand-dug, stone-lined well, and wells determined the location of headquarters, hospitals and aid stations | =Most farms had at least one well, which was hand-dug and lined with stone.

# --- Area 1 (First Day, Union 1st Corps) ---
102 | woodlots | Herr's Ridge Woods: two woodlots | - | 1863-07 | stated | At the time of the battle two separate woodlots formed Herr's Ridge Woods, each running roughly north-south | =At the time of the battle, two separate woodlots formed Herr’s Ridge Woods with the longest dimension of each running in a roughly north-south direction.
109 | fences | Forney farm: 3,891 fence rails burned (worm fencing) | - | 1863-07 | stated | John S. Forney's damage claim cited the burning of 3,891 fence rails, a number the report says is indicative of worm fencing | =John S. Forney filed damage claims after the battle and cited the burning of 3,891 fence rails.32 The high number of rails is indicative of worm fencing
109 | fences | Wills farm: 800 fence panels; fence on Wills Farm Lane shown on the 1863 Cope map | - | 1863-07 | stated | James J. Wills's claim cited 800 fence panels burned, destroyed or carried off to the line of battle, and the report adds that the fencing is depicted along the Wills Farm Lane on the 1863 Cope map and visible in a photograph of about the 1880s | =After the battle, James J. Wills filed damaged claims citing 800 panels of fence were burned, destroyed, or carried off to the line of battle.33 In addition, the fencing is depicted along the Wills Farm Lane on the 1863 Cope Map and is visible in a circa 1880s photograph
111 | fences | Worm fence on the east boundary of McPherson Field 7 (wheat) made into a breastwork | bld-mcpherson-farm | 1863-07-01 | stated | An account quoted by the report says the rails were carried from a fence on the eastern boundary of a field of wheat (McPherson Field 7) and from the pike to an old railroad cut, and the report says this worm fencing, repurposed into a breastwork, covered the Union color guard | =The rails had been carried together from a fence that formed the eastern boundary of a field of wheat [McPherson Field 7], extending down its western slope, and from the pike to an old R. R. cut north of it.”39 The worm fencing, repurposed into a breastwork, provided cover and concealment for the Union color guard.

# --- Area 2 (First Day, Union 11th Corps) ---
145 | damage | Josiah Benner farm: crop losses | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Josiah Benner family claimed two acres of corn destroyed by the 11th Corps and, with no command named, 30 acres of wheat in two fields, 22-26 acres of oats and two acres of barley | =the Josiah Benner family cited the 11th Corps for destruction of two acres of corn. No specific army command was cited for additional damage to or destruction of 30 acres of wheat in two separate fields, 22–26 acres of oats, and two acres of barley.
146 | woodlots | David Blocher Woods: grazed deciduous woodlot with scarce understory | - | 1863-07 | stated | Grazing by cattle and hogs left the understory of the David Blocher deciduous woodlot scarce at the time of the battle, a character the report says is confirmed by the 1863 Bachelder map, the 1872 survey and Boardman photographs | =Grazing by cattle and hogs within the deciduous woodlot resulted in scarce understory at the time of the battle.7 The character of the David Blocher Woods is confirmed in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the Boardman Collection
147 | orchards | McClean orchard documented in the 1863 Bachelder map | - | 1863-07 | stated | The McClean Orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map and in photographs from the Boardman and park collections | =The McClean Orchard provided Confederate cover and is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map and in photographs from the Boardman Collection and the park’s historical collection
147 | orchards | Gilbert orchard young or immature in 1863 | - | 1863-07 | hedged | The park's 1863 period plan notes that the Gilbert Orchard was young or immature at the time of the battle, from a difference between historic image sources | =Based on this difference in historic imagery sources, the park’s 1863 period plan notes the Gilbert Orchard was a young or immature orchard at the time of the battle.
149 | fences | Moses McClean fence losses: 4,546 rails, 35 posts, 10 board panels, stone fence injured | - | 1863-07 | stated | Moses McClean's claim cited injury to stone fence and the loss of 4,546 rails, 35 posts and 10 panels of board fence, a rail-to-post ratio the report says seems to indicate that most of the fencing was worm | =In his application for battle damage compensation for his farm, Moses McClean claimed injury to stone fence and the loss of 4546 rails, 35 posts, and 10 panels of board fence.17 The high number of rails in proportion to the number of posts seems to indicate that the majority of this fencing was worm
149 | fences | Worm fence on the south and west of McClean Thicket and Field 1 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The worm fencing along the south and west perimeters of McClean Thicket and Field 1 is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map (southern leg) and the 1868 Warren map | =Worm fencing along the south and west perimeters of McClean Thicket and Field 1 is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map [ID 1331 southern leg] and the 1868 Warren Map (Figure 86).
150 | fences | Modern four-rail fence unlike the five-rail 1863 type | - | 2000s reconstruction (modern) | stated | The report says the modern fence uses four rails instead of the usual five rails of the 1863 fence type | =This modern fencing consists of four rails instead of the usual five rails associated with the 1863 fence type.

# --- Area 3 (Pickett's Charge: Pitzer-McMillan-Bliss) ---
166 | fields | McDannell Field 1: open, Confederates built breastworks on its east side | - | 1863-07 | stated | McDannell's petition cited rifle pits and breastworks made on his land by the Confederates, and the report says the open character of McDannell Field 1 made the Confederates build breastworks on the east side of the field next to the orchard | =Henry McDannell’s petition for compensation included damage to his land by the Confederates “making rifle pits and breast works on it.”6 During the battle, the open character of McDannell Field 1 necessitated that the Confederates erect breastworks on east side of field bordering orchard for cover.
167 | fields | Russell Corn Field planted in corn, crop immature | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Russell Corn Field was planted in corn at the time of the battle, an immature crop that did not hinder troop movement or fields of fire | =Planted in corn at the time of the battle, the immature crop would not have interfered with troop movement or with fields of fire and made hazardous and deadly any prolonged occupation of the field.
171 | orchards | McMillan orchard and nursery: fruit trade, canned fruit | bld-mcmillan-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The file says the McMillan claimant was in the fruit trade and had a large quantity of canned fruit on hand, taken or destroyed with everything edible or portable on the premises | =The file states that the “claimant was engaged in the fruit trade, and had a large quantity of canned fruit on hand which, with everything else edible, or portable, on the premises, was taken away or destroyed.”
174 | fences | Post and rail fencing along the McDannell Orchard and Field 2 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The post and rail fencing along the boundaries of McDannell Orchard and Field 2, just east of the main Confederate battle line, is documented in Tipton Collection and park photographs | =McDannell Orchard and Field 2 are bounded on the north by the former National Guard Armory, on the east by the park boundary, on the south by McMillan Orchard and Nursery, and on the west by McDannell Field 1. Located immediately east of the main Confederate battle line, fences defining the boundaries of the orchard and field may have provided cover, been repurposed into breastworks, or created an obstacle to any eastward advance.19 Post and rail fencing along the orchard and field is documented in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
175 | fences | Stake-and-rider (worm) fence on the west boundary of Russell's corn field: 333 feet destroyed | - | 1863-07 | stated | 333 feet of stake and rider (worm) fencing destroyed in the battle, 20 panels on Dr. Barrett's line and 49 on David McMillan's, both abutting Russell's west boundary; the report says worm fencing is documented in the 1872 survey, park maps and Tipton photographs | =The 333 feet of stake and rider [worm] fencing destroyed in the battle included 20 panels along Dr. William Barrett’s line and 49 panels along David McMillan’s line, both of which abutted Russell’s west boundary.23 This fencing type provided cover to Confederate forces having been removed and repurposed into breastworks along Seminary Ridge.24 Worm fencing is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map, maps in the park’s historical collection, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection
176 | fences | Russell's south boundary post and rail fence: 55 panels | - | 1863-07 | stated | Russell claimed the loss of 55 panels of post and rail fence along his south boundary with Jacob Culp's property | =Russell claimed the loss of 55 panels of post and rail fence along his south boundary with Jacob Culp’s property.
176 | fences | Post and rail fencing around the Russell corn field documented in photographs | - | 1863-07 | stated | The post and rail fencing of the Sheely or Jacob Ziegler Field and Russell Corn Field is documented in park photographs | =The post and rail fencing is documented in photographs from the park’s historical collection (Figure 115).
176 | fences | Post and rail fence rebuilt 2008-2009 on the Russell Corn Field | - | 2008-2009 reconstruction (modern) | stated | In 2008 the park installed 552 feet and in 2009 947 feet of post and rail fence on the Russell Corn Field boundaries | =In 2009, the park installed 947 feet of post and rail fence along the east boundary of the Sheely or Jacob Ziegler Field and Russell Corn Field (Figure 116).
178 | fences | Jacob Culp field fences: 29 new panels and 72 panels lost; post and rail on the 1863 Bachelder map | - | 1863-07 | stated | Jacob Culp's claim cited fencing torn down on three sides of his corn field (29 panels of new fence and 72 more, partly post and rail), and the report says the post and rail fence lines are documented in the 1863 Bachelder map | =Jacob Culp claimed losses of fencing around his field “out the ‘long lane’.” Fencing on three sides of his corn field was torn down and included 29 panels of new fence as well as another 72 panels of fence (partly post and rail fence).30 The post and rail fencing created an obstacle to Confederate forces headed east toward Cemetery Ridge and the post and rail fence lines are documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map.
178 | fences | Jacob Culp field fence type documented in the 1868 Warren map | - | 1863-07 | stated | The post and rail fence type of the Jacob Culp Field is documented in the 1868 Warren map and in park photographs | =The post and rail fence type is documented in the 1868 Warren Map and in photographs from the park’s historical collection
180 | fences | Myers Fields 1 and 2 post and rail fence lines (entry heading 'Myers Fields 1 and 2 Post and Rail Fence Installation') | - | 1863-07 | stated | Under that heading the report says the post and rail fence lines are documented in the 1863 Bachelder map and the post and rail type in the 1868 Warren map and park photographs | =The post and rail fence lines are documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map. The post and rail fence type is documented in the 1868 Warren Map and in photographs from the park’s historical collection
183 | fences | Jacob Benner Corn Field south boundary worm fence | - | 1863-07 | stated | The division between the Jacob Benner Corn Field and Codori Field 3 is marked by a worm fence, which the report says is documented in the 1872 survey and park photographs | =Jacob Benner Corn Field is bounded on the south by Codori Field 3 and the division between these two fields is marked by a worm fence. This fence likely provided occasional cover for Union and Confederate soldiers from skirmishers and pickets and may also have been an obstacle to alignment during the advance by Confederates on July 3 as they used the Jacob Benner Corn Field as an avenue of approach.40 The worm fencing is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the park’s historical collection

# --- Area 4 (Defense of Cemetery Ridge) ---
212 | fields | William Patterson fields: crops and meadow in 1863 | - | 1863-07 | stated | The woody growth of today covers an open area that held crops and meadow at the time of the battle | =The woody growth obscured an open area that contained crops and meadow at the time of the battle.
219 | buildings | Patterson farmstead: log house and stable south of the orchard | - | 1863-07 | stated | At the time of the battle a log house and stable stood south of the Patterson orchard | =At the time of the battle, a log house and stable stood south of the orchard.
220 | orchards | William Patterson orchard: trees lost, 50 bushels of apples, orchard marked on his claim sketch | - | 1863-07 | stated | Patterson's claim cited loss of trees in his orchard and 50 bushels of apples, and his sketch located the orchard in the corner of the field north of the barn | =Patterson specifically claimed the loss, injury, or destruction of the trees in his orchard and to 50 bushels of apples.
220 | orchards | Wigert (Patterson) orchard documented in the 1863 Bachelder map | - | 1863-07 | stated | The orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map, the 1872 survey and photographs | =Additionally, the orchard is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map, 1872 topographical survey map, and in photographs from the Tipton Collection and the park’s historical collection
221 | orchards | Fruit gardens: domestic fruit for canning, cider, feed or sale | - | 1863-07 | stated | Farms of the time used fruit garden fruit for canning, cider and other drinks, livestock feed or sale for cash | =Fruit gardens were intended for domestic as opposed to commercial production; however, farms at the time of the battle would use the fruit grown for canning, to prepare cider and other drinks, to add to livestock feed, or if surplus, to sell to create cash revenue.
223 | fences | Peter Frey fence losses: rails, posts, 70 rods of post and rail, paling, boards, 2 gates | - | 1863-07 | stated | Frey's claim cited 5,436 large rails, 550 posts (?), 454 chestnut posts, 70 rods (1,155 feet) of post and rail fence, 336 feet of fencing boards, 26 feet of paling fence, 877 and 2,270 rails and 2 gates | =These losses embraced 5436 large rails, 550 posts (?), 454 chestnut posts in fence, 70 rods (1155 feet) of post and rail fence, 336 feet of fencing boards, 26 feet of paling fence, 877 rails in fence, 2270 rails in fence, and 2 gates.
223 | earthworks | Union soldiers used Frey's fencing to build breastworks | E-17 | 1863-07 | stated | The report quotes Frey's claim that the soldiers used the fencing to erect breastworks extending through the land | =The farm was within Union lines and the soldiers used the fencing to erect breastworks “extending through the land…some of which was carried on the land of other persons, and of course after the battle was claimed by them.”
227 | fences | Hummelbaugh yard and garden picket fences | bld-hummelbaugh-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The picket fences around the Hummelbaugh yard and garden are documented in the 1872 survey and Tipton photographs; the report found no specific reference to their effect on the battle | =Specific references to the how the picket fences around the Hummelbaugh yard and garden impacted the battle have not been discovered.40 The picket fencing is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the Tipton Collection
230 | fences | Trostle woodlot west boundary worm fence | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The worm fence on the west boundary of the Trostle woodlot (bounded north by Codori Woodlot, east by Wigert Fields 3 and 7, south by the John Weikert farm, west by Trostle Fields 11 and 12) is documented in the 1868 Warren map | =Trostle Woodlot is bounded on the north by Codori Woodlot, on the east by Wigert Fields 3 and 7, on the south by the John Weikert Farm complex, and on the west by Trostle Fields 11 and 12. The woodlot and the worm fence marking its west boundary provided cover and concealment for Union infantry en route to and from the fighting in the Wheatfield area on July 2.47 The worm fencing is documented in the 1868 Warren Map
270 | fences | Five-rail panels consistent with historic post and rail fences south of Brian Field 1 | bld-brian-farm | 2000s reconstruction (modern) | stated | The reconstructed panels contain five horizontal rails and are consistent with the historic post and rail fences separating Brian Field 1 from the fields to the south | =The fence panels contain five horizontal rails and are consistent with the historic post and rail fences separating Brian Field 1 from the adjacent fields to the south (Figure 199).
271 | fences | Frey claims: 70 rods (1,155 feet) of post and rail fence; orchard east boundary fence | - | 1863-07 | stated | Peter Frey's damage claims sought compensation for destroyed fence posts, rails and 70 rods (1,155 feet) of post and rail fence, referred to soldiers using fencing for breastworks, and the report says the orchard's east boundary post and rail fence is documented in the 1872 survey and park photographs | =Peter Frey’s damage claims filed after the battle seek compensation for destroyed fence posts, fence rails, and 70 rods or 1155 feet of post and rail fence. His claims specifically reference soldiers using fencing to erect breastworks “extending through the land…some of which was carried on the land of other persons, and of course after the battle was claimed by them.”6 The orchard’s east boundary fence likely created an obstacle to Union troop movements and could have been repurposed to provide cover as part of a breastwork. The post and rail fence is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the park’s historical collection

# --- Area 9 and 10 (Codori, Spangler, Staub, Sherfy; Emmitsburg Road Ridge) ---
358 | fences | Henry Spangler orchard worm fence | - | 1863-07 | stated | The Henry Spangler Orchard fence is documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder map and as a worm fence in the 1868 Warren map and 1872 survey | =Henry Spangler Orchard is bounded on the north by Henry Spangler Fields 1 and 2, on the east by Henry Spangler Field 3, on the south by Henry Spangler Farm Lane, and on the west by Henry Spangler Field 1. Confederates used the Henry Spangler Orchard for cover and the worm fencing likely improved the cover afforded by the orchard.35 This fencing type is documented as a wooden fence in the 1863 Bachelder Map and as a worm fence in the 1868 Warren Map and 1872 topographical survey map
360 | fences | Worm fence dividing Wentz and Sherfy Field 4 | bld-wentz-farm,bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The worm fence dividing Wentz and Sherfy Field 4 is documented in the 1868 Warren map and was likely dismantled to ease troop movement | =The worm fence dividing Wentz and Sherfy Field 4, GIS ID 1054, was likely dismantled to facilitate troop movement across these spaces.40 This fencing type is documented in the 1868 Warren Map
362 | fences | Sherfy yards picket fences and gates | bld-sherfy-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The picket fences defining the Sherfy yard areas are documented in Tipton and Boardman photographs and an 1869 Leslie's woodcut | =The Sherfy Farm buildings complex provided cover and created an obstacle for both forces. The picket fences defining the yard areas increased available cover and potential obstacles to troop movement.47 This fencing type is documented in photographs from the Tipton Collection, the Boardman Collection, and an 1869 woodcut from Leslie’s Illustrated Newspaper
396 | fences | Codori thicket south boundary worm fence (entry heading 'Codori Thicket South Boundary Worm Fence Installation') | bld-codori-farm | 1863-07 | stated | Under that heading the report says the fence is documented in the 1863 Bachelder map and the 1868 Warren map and obstructed Union troops | =This direction was continued for two hundred yards or so, when it brought the right of the line to the edge of some marshy ground, and the left near a fence.”14 This fencing type created an obstacle to Union troops and is documented in the 1863 Bachelder Map [GIS ID 1338] and in the 1868 Warren Map [GIS ID 1339]
406 | fences | Trostle-Wigert Lane fence (Trostle Field 10 south boundary) | bld-trostle-farm | 1863-07 | stated | The post and rail fence along the Trostle-Wigert Lane, which Union forces used to reach battle sites from Taneytown Road, is documented in the 1872 survey and Tipton photographs | =The Trostle-Wigert Lane was used by Union forces to reach battle sites and positions from Taneytown Road. This avenue of approach was a significant movement corridor for Union artillery and infantry throughout the last two days of the battle and was defended aggressively to prevent Confederate forces from attaining the Union rear. The fencing likely provided cover to Union forces traveling on the lane. Additionally the fence likely became an obstacle to lateral movement across the lane by both forces during the course of the battle.34 Post and rail fencing is documented in the 1872 topographical survey map and in photographs from the Tipton Collection
